Every website on the internet lives somewhere. When someone types your web address into a browser, their device connects to a server — a computer that stores your website files and delivers them on demand. Website hosting is the service that provides that server and keeps your site accessible around the clock.

## What hosting actually does

Your website is made up of files — pages, images, code, databases. Without hosting, those files have nowhere to live. A hosting provider stores them on a server connected to the internet, so your site can be reached by anyone, anywhere, at any time.

When someone visits your website, their browser sends a request to the server, the server retrieves the relevant files, and the page loads in their browser. This all happens in a fraction of a second with good hosting.

## What makes hosting good or bad

Not all hosting is equal. The quality of your hosting affects:

**Speed** — how quickly pages load for visitors. A slow host means a slow website, which frustrates visitors and hurts your [Google rankings](/blog/does-your-website-host-affect-your-google-rankings/).

**Uptime** — how reliably your site stays online. Poor hosting can mean unexpected downtime — times when your site is completely unreachable.

**Security** — whether your site is protected from attacks. Good hosting includes firewalls, malware scanning, and an SSL certificate as standard.

**Backups** — whether your site is backed up regularly so it can be restored if something goes wrong.

## Managed hosting vs doing it yourself

You can buy hosting directly from a provider and manage it yourself, or you can use a managed hosting service where someone handles the technical side for you. Managed hosting costs more but removes the complexity — no server configuration, no security updates to run, no troubleshooting when something goes wrong.

Read more about the difference in [managed hosting vs DIY hosting](/blog/managed-hosting-vs-diy-hosting/).

